```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16
diagnosis_text:
The fault is not a physical link failure. The mswitch0↔router16 EtherChannel
bond (eth0 r16_ms0 and eth1 r16_ms0_2 on both sides) is fully UP with traffic
flowing in both directions, and the 10.2.2.0/24 inter-device subnet is intact.
The team's hypothesis about a downed uplink is incorrect.

The actual root cause is that router16's FRR configuration file
(/etc/frr/frr.conf) is missing entirely — it was deleted or never written
during the maintenance event. Because FRR requires this file to exist and be
owned by the frr user before it will launch ospfd, the FRR service failed on
startup (systemctl reports Active: failed). As a result, only zebra, mgmtd, and
staticd are running; ospfd never came up. Router16's routing table contains
only its two directly-connected networks (10.2.2.0/24 via bond0 and
172.30.1.0/24 via eth2) and zero OSPF-learned routes. Router7 confirms this:
it has no OSPF neighbor at 172.30.1.1 (router16's eth2 address).

Campus 1 PCs (VLAN 10/20/30) send traffic to mswitch0 as their gateway, which
forwards upstream to router16 via the 10.2.2.0/24 link. Router16 then has no
route to any Campus 2 or backbone prefix and drops all such packets, causing
the reported outage. Intra-Campus-1 traffic works because it is handled
entirely within mswitch0's VLAN interfaces.

Corrective fix: Restore /etc/frr/frr.conf on router16 with the correct OSPF
configuration (interface statements for bond0 and eth2, OSPF process with
appropriate network statements and router-id), ensure the file is owned by
frr:frr with correct permissions, then run `systemctl restart frr` on router16.
=== END ===
```